# Line endings. Dorc's product IS sh, and a CRLF shebang is an exec failure the remote kernel
# raises before any shell exists — unguardable by anything we could ship (`plans/139` §5). So the
# repository must not be able to accrete CRLF by accident, and that guarantee cannot live in each
# contributor's `core.autocrlf`: that is per-machine config, and a checkout without it commits
# CRLF unopposed.
#
# `eol=lf` (not bare `text=auto`) also forces LF in the WINDOWS working tree. That is right here:
# the Windows toolchain is git-bash/dash and the acceptance corpus executes the sh it renders, so
# a CRLF working tree would be hostile to the thing this repo does.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ...except fixture material that carries the wrong ending ON PURPOSE, so the CRLF refusal has
# something real to refuse. Pinned by EXTENSION rather than by directory, so the exemption cannot
# silently widen to cover real source; `binary` additionally stops an editor or a diff view
# "helpfully" normalising the one file whose entire purpose is to be wrong.
*.crlf binary
